{"duration_api_ms":64850,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"f24eafd4-99b2-4259-bd90-72d792ab1e9e","total_cost_usd":0.45878379999999996,"usage":{"input_tokens":16,"cache_creation_input_tokens":34342,"cache_read_input_tokens":258719,"output_tokens":6612,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1154},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":34342,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":893,"cache_read_input_tokens":44440,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2106,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2106},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":16,"outputTokens":6612,"cacheReadInputTokens":258719,"cacheCreationInputTokens":34342,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.45878379999999996,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":1154,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":8,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"I found one real security bug in `lxml_html_clean.clean`. Using CSS escapes in the at-rule name lets `@import` get past the default cleaner. I wrote the report at `runs/lxml-html-clean_r4/work/bug_report_lxml_html_clean_clean_2026-10-06_02-30_m0bh.md`. I haven't applied or tested the fix it proposes.\n\n**What happens**\n\n- **Input and output:** the default `clean_html` removes a plain `@import` from a `<style>` block, but escaped versions come back unchanged:\n  ```\n  <style>@import url(http://evil.example/x.css);</style>   ->  <style> url(...);</style>\n  <style>@impor\\74  url(http://evil.example/x.css);</style> ->  unchanged\n  <style>@\\69mport url(http://evil.example/x.css);</style>  ->  unchanged\n  ```\n- **Why it matters:** browsers decode these escapes, so they still read the rule as `@import` and load the attacker's stylesheet. The code's own comment says this must not happen (\"The imported CSS can do anything; we just can't allow\").\n- **Cause:** `_replace_css_import` only matches the literal text `@\\s*import`. `_has_sneaky_javascript` deletes backslashes instead of decoding the escapes, so `@\\69mport` becomes `@69mport` and the check misses it. The same gap should also let escaped `expression(` and `javascript:` through in `<style>` content, though I only tested `@import`.\n- **Severity:** High; the bug type is Logic (security).\n- **Suggested fix:** decode CSS escapes before the checks in `_has_sneaky_javascript` runs; the patch is in the report.\n\n**What I tested**\n\n- **`@import` in `<style>` tags:** failed. Hypothesis found `@impor\\74 ` within a few examples.\n- **`@import` in `style` attributes:** passed. The default settings remove the whole `style` attribute.\n- **`javascript:`, `vbscript:` and `jscript:` links** with whitespace or entities mixed in: passed.\n- **Idempotence (cleaning twice gives the same result):** failed. The input `<html><p>` gives `<div><body><p></p></body></div>`, and cleaning that removes the leftover `<body>`. The library never promises idempotence, so I didn't report this.\n\nI also noticed that the default cleaner keeps `<base href=\"http://evil.com/\">`, but the code never claims to remove it, so I didn't report it either.\n\nThe tests are in `work/test_clean_props.py` and a minimal reproduction is in `work/repro.py`.","ttft_ms":2105,"type":"result","duration_ms":70075,"uuid":"fcbad8f5-4